from qb import Q, O

Q("P1-Q01", "1.7",
  "A support agent resumes a named session two days later when a customer returns about order #55120. The saved history contains a lookup_order result showing the order as 'processing'. Since then the warehouse shipped it, but the resumed agent tells the customer the order has not shipped yet. The reply is well formed and polite, but it is wrong. What is the most reliable way to prevent this?",
  [O("Start a new session seeded with a structured summary of confirmed case facts, and call lookup_order again for current order state.", True,
     "Correct. Prior tool results are stale, so a fresh session with an injected summary of durable facts plus a live lookup gives the agent accurate state."),
   O("Resume the same session and add a system prompt line saying that earlier tool results may be outdated and should be treated cautiously.", False,
     "The stale 'processing' result remains in context and the model must guess which earlier results to distrust. A caution line is probabilistic and does not refresh the data."),
   O("Fork the session with fork_session and ask the forked branch to answer from the saved analysis without further tool calls.", False,
     "A fork starts from the same history, including the stale result. Forking is for exploring divergent approaches, not for refreshing outdated data."),
   O("Switch to a model with a larger context window so that the newest user message outweighs older tool output in the history.", False,
     "Context size does not change which facts are true. Older tool results do not become less influential simply because the window is larger.")],
  "When most of what a session remembers is tool output that can change (order status, shipment state), resuming it carries stale facts forward. The guide's guidance is that starting a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale tool results. The summary keeps durable facts (customer ID, order number, what the customer asked for) and the agent re-queries volatile state before answering.",
  "Session resumption versus fresh start with injected summary (Task 1.7).",
  "The elapsed two days and the changed warehouse status are the decisive details: the problem is stale tool results, not model capability or prompt wording. Only an option that refreshes the data fixes the root cause.",
  "Treat tool results as a cache with a lifetime. On resume, carry forward durable case facts and re-fetch anything that can change.")

Q("P1-Q02", "1.4",
  "Logs show the agent correctly calls get_customer and receives verified customer ID C-1042. The caller then asks for a refund on order #88231, and the agent calls process_refund for it. An audit later finds order #88231 belongs to customer C-7710. The team already has a hook that blocks process_refund until get_customer has succeeded in the session. What change best closes this gap?",
  [O("In a PreToolUse hook for process_refund, load the order and reject the call unless its customer ID equals the verified customer ID for the session.", True,
     "Correct. This enforces authorization (the verified person owns this order) deterministically, at the point the side effect would occur."),
   O("Extend the existing prerequisite so it also requires lookup_order to have been called earlier in the session for the same order number.", False,
     "Calling lookup_order only proves the order exists. It does not prove that the verified customer owns it, so the unauthorized refund still passes."),
   O("Update the system prompt to tell the agent that it must confirm an order belongs to the verified customer before issuing a refund.", False,
     "This relies on the model remembering to perform the check every time. Financial side effects need a guarantee, and prompts have a non-zero failure rate."),
   O("Ask the caller to restate the email address on the account before each refund and compare it to the get_customer record in the prompt.", False,
     "Restating an email is another identity check (authentication). The failure here is that a verified identity acted on an order it does not own.")],
  "Authentication (who is this person) and authorization (are they allowed to act on this resource) are different checks. The existing gate only proves that authentication happened. A tool-call interception hook for process_refund, or equivalent server-side validation, must compare the order's owner to the verified customer and block mismatches. Doing so deterministically avoids depending on the model's compliance.",
  "Programmatic enforcement of authorization before a financial side effect (Task 1.4, 1.5).",
  "The audit shows a verified customer ID and a refund on someone else's order. That is an ownership mismatch, so the correct fix must compare the order's customer to the verified customer, not merely add another identity step or another reminder.",
  "Gate every mutating tool on resource-level ownership checks, enforced in code or in the tool backend. Do not stop at 'the user was verified'.")

Q("P1-Q03", "1.1",
  "A developer wrote the agent loop to stop as soon as the assistant message contains any text block, and also caps the loop at 5 iterations. Customers sometimes see only 'Let me check that order for you' and nothing happens. Some complex billing disputes end abruptly after the fifth iteration with no resolution. Which redesign best fixes both symptoms?",
  [O("Continue while stop_reason is 'tool_use', executing every requested tool and appending the results, and finish when stop_reason is 'end_turn'. Keep an iteration limit only as a backstop that escalates.", True,
     "Correct. The stop_reason is the authoritative signal. A backstop that escalates handles runaway loops without being the normal way the loop ends."),
   O("Keep the text check, but instruct the model never to write any text before calling a tool so the first text block always signals completion.", False,
     "This still treats assistant text as a completion indicator and depends on the model obeying a prompt rule. Text can legitimately appear alongside tool_use blocks."),
   O("Raise the iteration cap to 25 and also stop the loop when the response includes a phrase such as 'I have completed your request'.", False,
     "Parsing natural-language phrases for termination is brittle, and a larger cap is still an arbitrary number acting as the primary stopping rule."),
   O("Replace the loop with a fixed sequence of get_customer, lookup_order, then process_refund so each request runs the same three steps.", False,
     "A fixed sequence removes model-driven decisions, so account issues and disputes that need other steps cannot be handled, and it still has no correct termination logic.")],
  "The loop should be driven by the API's stop_reason: when it is 'tool_use', run the requested tools, append the tool_result blocks to the conversation, and call the model again; when it is 'end_turn', return the final response. Assistant text can accompany tool_use, so checking for text ends the loop too early. A hard cap used as the main terminator cuts off legitimate multi-step cases; it belongs only as a safety backstop, ideally escalating to a human when reached.",
  "Agentic loop control flow and anti-patterns (Task 1.1).",
  "Two symptoms appear together: early exit when text is present, and abrupt exit at five iterations. Both come from using the wrong termination signals (text content and a cap), so the fix must replace both with stop_reason-based control.",
  "Never infer completion from message text or an arbitrary count. Use stop_reason, and make caps a guard that fails safely.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q04", "1.5",
  "The agent evaluates a 30-day return window using data from three MCP tools: get_customer returns account creation as a Unix timestamp, lookup_order returns ISO 8601 dates, and a legacy billing tool returns payment status as numeric codes (1, 2, 3). The agent sometimes miscalculates eligibility and misreads billing status. What is the most robust fix?",
  [O("Add a PostToolUse hook that converts all dates to one format and maps numeric status codes to labeled values before the model sees any tool result.", True,
     "Correct. A hook normalizes heterogeneous results deterministically, so the model always reasons over consistent data."),
   O("Describe the conversion formula for Unix timestamps and the meaning of each status code in the system prompt and ask the agent to convert before reasoning.", False,
     "The model must then perform conversions on every call, and any lapse produces wrong eligibility decisions. It is probabilistic where the problem needs determinism."),
   O("Lengthen each tool's description to explain the format of its output fields so the model knows how to interpret each response.", False,
     "Better descriptions help selection and interpretation, but they still leave the arithmetic and mapping to the model on every call."),
   O("Add a convert_timestamp tool and instruct the agent to call it whenever a date appears in any tool result.", False,
     "This adds extra tool calls, tokens, and more chances to skip a call. The normalization is mechanical and should not depend on model initiative.")],
  "PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results before the model processes them, which makes them the right place to normalize timestamps and status codes. The model then compares dates in one format and reads labeled statuses, and the transformation is guaranteed rather than requested.",
  "PostToolUse hooks for data normalization (Task 1.5).",
  "The tools return inconsistent formats and the failures are intermittent, which points to model-side conversion errors. A deterministic transformation layer is better than asking the model to convert correctly each time.",
  "Normalize at the integration boundary. Keep the model's input consistent and let code do code's work.")

Q("P1-Q05", "1.6",
  "A customer writes one message: they want to return a blender from order #4410, dispute a $39 charge that appears tied to the same order, and update a shipping address. The return refund affects the amount that is actually in dispute. Which design resolves the message quickly while avoiding duplicate or conflicting refunds?",
  [O("Split the message into three concerns, run read-only investigation for them in parallel from shared verified-customer and order facts, reconcile the findings, then execute any refund once.", True,
     "Correct. It parallelizes the safe, independent lookups, preserves shared context, and makes the one side-effect decision after the interacting facts are combined."),
   O("Launch three isolated subagents that each handle one concern end to end, including calling process_refund if their analysis supports it.", False,
     "Isolated agents do not share facts. The return and the dispute concern the same charge, so both could issue refunds for it."),
   O("Process the concerns strictly in the order mentioned, starting each from a clean context so earlier issues cannot influence later ones.", False,
     "This is slower and discards the shared facts that matter. The later dispute depends on what happened with the return."),
   O("Escalate the entire message to a human agent because it contains several issues and one of them involves money.", False,
     "Multi-concern requests are within the agent's remit. Escalating reduces first-contact resolution when the issues can be resolved with the available tools.")],
  "The guide describes decomposing multi-concern requests into distinct items and investigating them in parallel using shared context before synthesizing a unified resolution. Because the return and dispute interact, the side effect (the refund) must happen once, after the facts are reconciled. Reads can be parallel; writes need coordination.",
  "Task decomposition with shared context and controlled side effects (Task 1.4, 1.6).",
  "The sentence 'the return refund affects the amount in dispute' shows the concerns interact. That rules out fully independent agents that can each refund, and it rules out an unnecessary escalation.",
  "Parallelize reads, serialize writes. Reconcile interacting facts before any irreversible action.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q06", "1.4",
  "The agent calls escalate_to_human with the note: 'Customer is upset, please help.' Human agents work in a separate ticketing system and cannot see the agent's conversation transcript. They must ask the customer to start over. What should the agent include to fix this?",
  [O("A structured handoff summary with customer ID, root cause, refund amount involved, and the recommended next action.", True,
     "Correct. The human can act immediately without the transcript."),
   O("The full raw conversation text and every tool output pasted into the escalation note as one large block.", False,
     "This buries the key facts in noise and includes sensitive or irrelevant data. A focused summary is easier to act on."),
   O("Only the customer's name, with an instruction for the human agent to find the case in the CRM by searching that name.", False,
     "Names are often ambiguous (multiple matches), and it still omits the cause, amounts, and recommendation."),
   O("A message asking the customer to describe the problem again to the human agent when the connection is made.", False,
     "That recreates the exact problem: the customer repeats themselves and context is lost.")],
  "Humans who lack transcript access depend on the handoff summary. The guide specifies compiling structured summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) when escalating mid-process so the human can resolve the case without re-investigating.",
  "Structured handoff protocols (Task 1.4).",
  "The stated constraint that humans cannot see the transcript means the escalation payload must carry all necessary facts itself, in a usable structure.",
  "Design handoffs as if the receiver has no other context, because often they do not.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q07", "1.7",
  "A support lead wants to compare two resolutions for a complex disputed order, a full refund versus store credit plus a partial refund. Both should start from the same completed investigation of the order history. A prototype lets both branches call process_refund while exploring. What approach is best?",
  [O("Use fork_session to create two branches from the shared investigation. Have each branch draft its proposed resolution without executing refunds, then execute only the chosen one once.", True,
     "Correct. Forks share the baseline, and keeping branches draft-only prevents duplicate or conflicting refunds."),
   O("Fork the session and let both branches execute their resolution, then reverse whichever one the lead does not choose.", False,
     "Executing both creates real side effects and customer-visible actions that may not be cleanly reversible."),
   O("Resume the same named session in two terminals with --resume and let each terminal pursue a different resolution.", False,
     "Both processes continue the same conversation lineage rather than creating independent branches, and each may execute actions."),
   O("Run strategy one in the current session and then instruct the agent to disregard it and try strategy two in the same session.", False,
     "The first strategy remains in context and biases the second, and any executed refund stays executed.")],
  "fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline so divergent approaches can be explored without contaminating each other. Because process_refund is a side effect, exploration should be draft-only, with execution happening once after a decision.",
  "fork_session for divergent exploration; side-effect safety (Task 1.7).",
  "The prototype's ability to refund in both branches is the hazard. The best answer keeps the benefit of forking while removing the duplicate-action risk.",
  "Exploration branches should be read-only or draft-only. Commit side effects once.")
